WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.194
Think of a relational dataset as a graph: rows are
vertices and references are edges Partitioning

00:00:06.194 --> 00:00:10.728
tries to balance the data while cutting few
frequently traversed edges.

00:00:10.728 --> 00:00:10.920
No.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:16.858
It can make profile reads local, but the rename now
affects many copies or requires accepting

00:00:16.858 --> 00:00:18.327
temporary disagreement.

00:00:18.327 --> 00:00:24.530
A deterministic hash maps a key to a value in a
fixed hash space Partition owners can divide that

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:30.286
space into ranges; a client or routing layer hashes
a requested key and selects its owner.

00:00:30.286 --> 00:00:30.477
No.

00:00:30.477 --> 00:00:36.681
All requests for that key still map to its owner or
replica set unless the application introduces

00:00:36.681 --> 00:00:39.302
another distribution or caching strategy.

00:00:39.327 --> 00:00:45.427
Partitioning spreads different data across machines;
replication stores multiple copies of each

00:00:45.427 --> 00:00:51.527
partition Replication allows a system to keep a copy
when a machine fails, but a change reaches

00:00:51.527 --> 00:00:53.325
replicas at different times.

00:00:53.325 --> 00:00:59.232
Not while also remaining available for every
request; they cannot coordinate the conflicting

00:00:59.232 --> 00:01:01.223
decisions across the partition.
